---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
---

<Layout
	title="Geometrical Meaning of Zeroes of a Polynomial | Class 10 Mathematics | The Concepts Adda"
	description="Learn how the zeroes of a polynomial represent X-axis intersections on the graph y = p(x) with linear and parabola curves for Class 10 NCERT."
>
	<main class="lesson-page">
		<div class="lesson-container">
			<a href="/polynomials" class="back-link">
				<span class="back-arrow">←</span>
				<span>Back to Polynomials</span>
			</a>

			<!-- LESSON HEADER -->
			<section class="lesson-header">
				<div class="lesson-number">02</div>
				<div class="lesson-header-copy">
					<div class="lesson-label">LESSON 02 · CLASS 10 MATHEMATICS (NCERT)</div>
					<h1>Geometrical Meaning of <span>Zeroes</span></h1>
					<p>
						Discover how the zeroes of any polynomial p(x) are visually represented by the x-coordinates of the points where the graph y = p(x) intersects the X-axis.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- CONTENT LAYOUT -->
			<section class="content-layout">
				<article class="lesson-content">

					<!-- 01 · THE GRAPHICAL CONCEPT -->
					<div class="topic-label">01 · GRAPHICAL INTERPRETATION</div>
					<h2>Graph of y = p(x) and the X-axis</h2>
					<p>
						For any polynomial <em>y = p(x)</em>, the <strong>zeroes</strong> are the <em>x-coordinates</em> of the points where the graph intersects or touches the <strong>X-axis</strong> (i.e. where <em>y = 0</em>).
					</p>

					<div class="idea-box">
						<div class="idea-icon">✓</div>
						<div class="idea-content">
							<strong>Key NCERT Rule</strong>
							<p>
								A polynomial <em>p(x)</em> of degree <em>n</em> intersects the X-axis at <strong>at most n points</strong>. Therefore, a polynomial of degree <em>n</em> has <strong>at most n zeroes</strong>.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- 02 · LINEAR & QUADRATIC GRAPHS -->
					<div class="topic-label second-label">02 · TYPES OF GRAPHS</div>
					<h2>Graphs of Linear & Quadratic Polynomials</h2>

					<!-- SVG Visualizing Parabola Intersections -->
					<div class="example-card">
						<div class="example-header">
							<span class="example-badge">VISUAL DIAGRAM</span>
							<h3>Graph of Quadratic Polynomial y = ax² + bx + c (Parabola)</h3>
						</div>
						<div class="example-solution">
							<p>The graph of a quadratic polynomial is a curve called a <strong>parabola</strong>. It can open upwards (a &gt; 0) or downwards (a &lt; 0):</p>
							
							<div style="margin:20px 0; text-align:center;">
								<svg width="340" height="180" viewBox="0 0 340 180" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="background:#f8fafc; border-radius:16px; border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">
									<!-- Axes -->
									<line x1="20" y1="120" x2="320" y2="120" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="2"/>
									<line x1="170" y1="20" x2="170" y2="160" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="2"/>
									<text x="325" y="125" fill="#64748b" font-size="12" font-weight="bold">X</text>
									<text x="165" y="15" fill="#64748b" font-size="12" font-weight="bold">Y</text>

									<!-- Parabola -->
									<path d="M 60 20 Q 170 170 280 20" stroke="#2563eb" stroke-width="3" fill="none"/>

									<!-- Intersection Points -->
									<circle cx="100" cy="120" r="6" fill="#e11d48"/>
									<circle cx="240" cy="120" r="6" fill="#e11d48"/>
									<text x="85" y="142" fill="#e11d48" font-size="13" font-weight="bold">(α, 0)</text>
									<text x="225" y="142" fill="#e11d48" font-size="13" font-weight="bold">(β, 0)</text>
								</svg>
							</div>

							<p>Here, the parabola cuts the X-axis at <strong>two points</strong> (α, 0) and (β, 0). Thus, the polynomial has <strong>2 distinct real zeroes: x = α and x = β</strong>.</p>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- 03 · THREE CASES FOR QUADRATIC GRAPHS -->
					<div class="topic-label second-label">03 · NCERT CASES</div>
					<h2>3 Possible Cases for Quadratic Graphs</h2>

					<div class="summary-list">
						<div class="summary-item">
							<span>Case 1</span>
							<div>
								<p><strong>Cuts X-axis at 2 distinct points</strong></p>
								<span style="color:#64748b; font-size:14px;">The polynomial has <strong>2 distinct zeroes</strong>.</span>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="summary-item">
							<span>Case 2</span>
							<div>
								<p><strong>Touches X-axis at exactly 1 point</strong></p>
								<span style="color:#64748b; font-size:14px;">The 2 points coincide, giving <strong>2 equal zeroes (1 unique zero)</strong>.</span>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="summary-item">
							<span>Case 3</span>
							<div>
								<p><strong>Remains completely above or below X-axis</strong></p>
								<span style="color:#64748b; font-size:14px;">Does not intersect X-axis, so it has <strong>0 real zeroes</strong>.</span>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- 04 · QUICK SUMMARY -->
					<div class="topic-label second-label">04 · QUICK SUMMARY</div>
					<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>

					<div class="summary-list">
						<div class="summary-item">
							<span>01</span>
							<p>Number of zeroes = Number of points where graph <em>y = p(x)</em> cuts/touches the <strong>X-axis</strong>.</p>
						</div>
						<div class="summary-item">
							<span>02</span>
							<p>Graph of a linear polynomial is a <strong>straight line</strong> (at most 1 zero).</p>
						</div>
						<div class="summary-item">
							<span>03</span>
							<p>Graph of a quadratic polynomial is a <strong>parabola</strong> (at most 2 zeroes).</p>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- LESSON NAVIGATION -->
					<div class="lesson-navigation">
						<a href="/polynomials/introduction" class="previous-lesson">
							<span>←</span>
							<div>
								<small>PREVIOUS LESSON</small>
								<strong>Introduction to Polynomials</strong>
							</div>
						</a>

						<a href="/polynomials/relationship-zeroes-coefficients" class="next-lesson">
							<div>
								<small>NEXT LESSON</small>
								<strong>Zeroes & Coefficients</strong>
							</div>
							<span>→</span>
						</a>
					</div>
				</article>

				<!-- SIDEBAR -->
				<aside class="lesson-sidebar">
					<div class="sidebar-card">
						<div class="sidebar-title">POLYNOMIALS</div>
						<div class="progress-text">Lesson 02 of 04</div>
						<div class="progress-bar">
							<div style="width: 50%;"></div>
						</div>

						<div class="sidebar-lessons">
							<a href="/polynomials/introduction" class="sidebar-lesson">
								<span>01</span>
								<div>
									<strong>Introduction</strong>
									<small>Degree, terms & zeroes definition</small>
								</div>
							</a>

							<a href="/polynomials/geometrical-meaning" class="sidebar-lesson active">
								<span>02</span>
								<div>
									<strong>Geometrical Meaning</strong>
									<small>Graphs of y = p(x) & X-axis</small>
								</div>
							</a>

							<a href="/polynomials/relationship-zeroes-coefficients" class="sidebar-lesson">
								<span>03</span>
								<div>
									<strong>Zeroes & Coefficients</strong>
									<small>Sum (α + β) & product (α · β)</small>
								</div>
							</a>

							<a href="/polynomials/pyqs" class="sidebar-lesson">
								<span>04</span>
								<div>
									<strong>Previous Year PYQs</strong>
									<small>CBSE 2020-2024 practice set</small>
								</div>
							</a>
						</div>
					</div>
				</aside>
			</section>
		</div>
	</main>

